      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Solve for $x$ where ${2}^{x-1}={16}^{x+1}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $16$ as ${2}^{4},$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x-1} & ={16}^{x+1} \\\\ {2}^{x-1} & ={({2}^{4})}^{x+1} \\\\ {2}^{x-1} & ={2}^{4(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ -5 & =3x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
